WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.3 · Financial Management
Project budgets, cost allocation and full cost recovery

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Your first project’s true cost includes a fair share of the costs that keep you running.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.504
A project budget shows the cost of one piece of work, such as a pilot or a first product line. It includes its direct costs and a fair share of the shared costs that make it possible.

4
00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:29.504
Two costs, one fair price

5
00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:41.204
Direct costs: Costs that exist only because this project runs, itemised as quantity × unit cost. For example: The bakery’s school pilot: flour, boxes, delivery fuel and the founder’s hours baking.

6
00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:54.385
Shared costs: Costs that keep the whole initiative running, shared out on a written basis such as time. For example: Bookkeeping, insurance and phone, shared by time: the pilot uses 30% of the founders’ hours.

7
00:00:54.385 --> 00:01:08.026
Full cost recovery: Each paid or funded project covers its fair share, so nothing else quietly fills the gap. For example: The pilot’s price includes 30% of shared costs, not just ingredients and delivery.

8
00:01:08.426 --> 00:01:09.026
Why the full cost matters

9
00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:14.700
1. Low overheads do not mean efficiency; often nobody pays for support.

10
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.004
2. Without shared costs, founders’ unpaid time quietly subsidises the project.

11
00:01:20.004 --> 00:01:26.678
3. A real shared-cost rate makes your prices and funding asks honest.

12
00:01:27.078 --> 00:01:27.678
Where you will use it

13
00:01:27.678 --> 00:01:32.981
1. Your prices for products or services, from the first sale.

14
00:01:32.981 --> 00:01:37.915
2. Your funding asks, within any limits a funder sets.

15
00:01:37.915 --> 00:01:45.330
3. Your organisational budget: project budgets add up to it, with nothing counted twice.

16
00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:46.330
What to avoid

17
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:52.004
Budgeting only direct costs because funders or customers see only those.

18
00:01:52.004 --> 00:01:57.307
Changing the cost-sharing method from one project to the next.

19
00:01:57.307 --> 00:02:02.870
Using different cost headings in each project budget.

20
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.270
You build a full-cost budget for your first project or pilot.

21
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:09.270
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

22
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.870
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
